chinese authorities have imposed their  most severe lockdown in almost a year  13 million people living in the northern  city of xi'an have been ordered to stay  home from midnight on thursday  only one member of each household is  allowed out to get food every two days  and only certified essential workers can  head out for work  the measures prompted crowds to rush to  grocery stores and markets earlier this  week  more than 200 cases have been reported  in the city since the beginning of the  month  the outbreak of the delta variant began  with just nine people and quickly spread  the trajectories of the night cases were  complex involving transportation  services and public places including  hospitals commercial complexes  restaurants and schools  hundreds of thousands of tourists flock  to xi'an a former capital and historic  hub to see the terracotta warriors every  year  now almost all transport in and out of  the area has been cancelled  residents must apply for special  permission to leave the city  authorities are currently conducting  their third round of mass testing  so far no cases of the omicron variant  have been locally transmitted in china  but officials here are on high alert the  beijing winter olympics opens in  february and chinese leaders have  promised a safe and secure games where  spectators will be able to attend  overseas spectators however won't be  attending china has maintained its zero  tolerance approach to the virus and  borders have been closed since march  last year  during the winter olympics there will be  a large number of olympic affiliated  individuals from different countries and  regions coming to china there is a high  possibility that a certain number of  kovi 19 positive cases will arise  more than 80 percent of the country's  population have received two doses of  coronavirus vaccines  officials are now encouraging people to  get a third shot  but some studies have found that chinese  vaccines have lower efficacy and  protection rates against the omicron  strain  beijing has not approved the use of some  other vaccines such as pfizer or  astrazeneca  katrina you al jazeera beijing
 

